**Idea.** The graph state (`finished`, `started`) is shared. Whenever a job finishes, the scheduler must (1) record it, (2) ask `get_next_jobs(finished)` for newly ready jobs, (3) start any not yet started. Keep that sequence inside one lock so two finishing jobs cannot produce duplicate or missed launches. The main thread waits until nothing is running.

```java
class JobScheduler {
  private final Set<Job> finished = new HashSet<>();
  private final Set<Job> started  = new HashSet<>();
  private int running = 0;
  private boolean failed = false;

  public synchronized void run() throws InterruptedException {
    launch(getNextJobs(finished));
    while (running > 0) wait();          // releases the lock while waiting
    if (failed) throw new IllegalStateException("pipeline failed");
  }

  private void launch(List<Job> jobs) {  // lock is held
    for (Job j : jobs) {
      if (!failed && started.add(j)) {   // add() returns false if already started
        running++;
        new Thread(() -> execute(j)).start();
      }
    }
  }

  private void execute(Job j) {
    boolean ok = true;
    try { j.doWork(); } catch (Exception e) { ok = false; }
    synchronized (this) {
      running--;
      if (ok) { finished.add(j); launch(getNextJobs(finished)); }
      else failed = true;
      notifyAll();
    }
  }
}
```

**Why it is correct.**
- `running` is incremented *before* the thread starts and while holding the lock, so the main loop cannot see 0 while a successor is about to be launched.
- `started` prevents launching a job twice if `get_next_jobs` keeps returning jobs that are already running or done.
- Work (`doWork`) runs outside the lock, so jobs run in parallel; only bookkeeping is serialised.
- On failure, no new jobs launch; the scheduler waits for running jobs to finish so none is left running.

**Improvements to discuss.** A thread per job can explode with big graphs: use a fixed set of worker `Thread`s pulling from a blocking queue (still no ExecutorService). Support cancellation by interrupting running threads. Detect cycles (no job ever becomes ready while nothing runs). Alternatives: Kahn's algorithm with an in-degree map and a decrement per finished job gives O(V+E) total instead of recomputing `get_next_jobs`.
